text:
maya: Thinking out loud — should we standardize on gRPC bidi streaming for internal console -> runtime telemetry + real-time tokens? Pros: binarized protobufs, lower CPU on parsing, simpler backpressure. Cons: browser support, intermediate gateways, and harder to proxy through some infra. Opinions?

jun: +1 for gRPC for internal services, but browser clients need gRPC-web or a websocket fallback. gRPC-web requires Envoy or proxy that supports it.

priyanka: Concerned about quotas and rate limiting. Our current quota infra expects HTTP semantics (paths/verbs). How would we map streaming RPCs to quota keys?

liam: We can attach metadata headers at stream start and use per-connection tokens. Envoy has rate limit filter for gRPC. But counting per-stream vs per-message needs careful design. Also proto evolution is easier if we keep backwards compatible oneofs.

sam: For public hosted API we should keep REST/JSON for broadest compatibility. Internally, teams that need perf can consume gRPC. That said, having two APIs multiplies maintenance surface unless we auto-transcode.

maya: Right. Transcoding (Envoy grpc-json-transcoder) helps for unary RPCs, but it doesn't handle bidi streaming. For streaming we need either: 1) websocket fronting that translates messages to gRPC on the backend, or 2) SSE/longpoll fallbacks that break streaming semantics.

jun: Websockets are a decent compromise for browsers. Browser -> ws -> gateway -> gRPC. Need to ensure auth and reconnect semantics are consistent.

priyanka: Also think about observability: telemetry events need consistent request IDs. If we have ws->gRPC translation, make sure we propagate traceparent/x-request-id on each message. Otherwise Console dashboards will show split traces.

liam: Agreed. We should define a small envelope proto that includes headers for trace/span/request_id and a payload union. That makes gateway translation stateless. Example proto snippet:```syntax = "proto3";
package redwood.runtime;
message StreamEnvelope {
  string request_id = 1;
  bytes payload = 2; // nested proto serialized depending on type
}
service RuntimeStream {
  rpc Connect(stream StreamEnvelope) returns (stream StreamEnvelope);
}
```

sam: Love the envelope idea, but we need strong typing for payloads for evals and model-aware tooling. Maybe payload uses `oneof` with known message types.

maya: Good call. Also rollout strategy: canary new gRPC endpoint behind feature flag for specific accounts. If websocket fallback fails, gateway should auto-fallback to a REST chunking endpoint. We should document fallback precedence: gRPC native -> gRPC-web -> websocket-> rest-chunked.

jun: One implementation pain: CORS and preflight for browsers when using gRPC-web + window.fetch. Envoy config is fiddly. Add integration tests for CORS, cookies, and bearer auth flows.

priyanka: For quotas: propose we keep per-token quota model and count per-message tokens using the same kernel we use for billing. For streams, gauge current inflight token consumption and apply leaky-bucket. We should also expose per-route token metrics to the Console.

liam: On proto evolution: add a `version` field at envelope level so gateway can do light translation for old clients. Also publish codegen for SDKs for both streaming and unary.

sam: Ops perspective: monitoring—expose these metrics: stream_open, stream_messages_in, stream_messages_out, stream_errors, per-account_token_usage. We'll hook these into existing per-route dashboards.

infra-bot: integration-tests/run-grpc-web passed (build #4578) :white_check_mark:

maya: Next actions: maya to sketch gateway translation patterns + envoys config; liam to draft envelope proto + codegen plan; priyanka + sam to pair on quota semantics and dashboard surface. I'll start a short RFC and a follow-up sync. :eyes:
